Hi Eric,

I had a look at Chapter 5 but, to be honest, it was generally above my comprehension level.  Thanks for Len’s R code in regards to Fig 5.6, that helps me sort things out.

I’m also interested in a comparison between abundance values from two different years’ data.  I followed the methods in section 3.6.5 from Introduction to Distance Sampling (Buckland et al. 2011).  Specifically, I followed the formula for a shared source of variance (equations 3.108 to 3.111).  I noted that this slightly increased the precision to detect differences when compared to the method that assumes complete independence between the two estimates (eqns 3.99 to 3.101).  Intuitively this did not make sense since I was used to variance calculations, that included common sources of variance, to slightly increase the CV when adding separate abundance estimates.  Conceptually, it makes sense that a common source of variance would increase the precision for determining the difference between two estimates but it would be nice to have some sort of affirmation that I haven’t messed up the calculations.

I would also assume that the calculation of degrees of freedom would also benefit by taking the same common sources of variance into account – is this what “Inference can now proceed as before, either with additional applications of Satterthwhaite’s approximation…” is referring to? (page 86 first paragraph)

Thanks,

Mitch

On Friday, 20 March 2020 01:56:02 UTC-7, Eric Rexstad wrote:

> Hello again Mitch
>
> Chapter 5 of the Advanced Distance Sampling text (Buckland et al. 2004) is entitled "Temporal inferences from distance sampling surveys".  That chapter discusses means of assessing power to detect change from a series of surveys.  The computations described therein are not dependent upon sample size determination, but rather upon precision in the resulting density or abundance estimates.
>
> There is companion R code used to create some of the figures in the chapter to be found here at Len Thomas' web site:
> http://lenthomas.org/software/rtrend.zip
>

>
> Eric Rexstad
> Senior Research Fellow, CREEM, Univ. of St Andrews, Charity SC013532

